## Key numbers

| Metric | Value | Definition |
|---|---|---|
| Revenue | {{ k.revenue | brl(2) }} | Sum of item prices, excluding freight ({{ k.freight | brl(2) }} freight on top) |
| Orders | {{ k.orders | num }} | Orders in the window, excluding canceled and unavailable |
| Customers | {{ k.customers | num }} | Distinct `customer_unique_id` |
| Average order value | {{ k.aov | money }} | Revenue / orders |
| Repeat purchase rate | {{ k.repeat_rate | pct(2) }} | Customers with 2+ orders / all customers |
| On-time delivery | {{ k.on_time_rate | pct }} | Delivered on or before the promised date |
| Average delivery time | {{ k.avg_delivery_days | sig(1) }} days | Purchase to customer receipt |
| Average review | {{ k.avg_review | sig }} / 5 | Latest review per order |

## What holds back growth, and three actions

| # | Action | Evidence | Estimated annual impact |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Second-purchase programme** aimed at Promising and At-Risk one-time buyers (post-delivery voucher, category-matched recommendations) | {{ a.rfm.high_value_one_timers | num }} high-value one-time buyers are {{ a.rfm.high_value_one_timers_customer_share | pct(0) }} of customers but {{ a.rfm.high_value_one_timers_revenue_share | pct(0) }} of revenue; month-1 retention is {{ a.cohort.month1_retention | pct(2) }} | **{{ i.a1_repeat_revenue | brl }}** if repeat rate rises {{ as_.repeat_lift_pp | sig(1) }} pp (range {{ i.a1_low | brl }} to {{ i.a1_high | brl }} for {{ as_.repeat_lift_low_pp | sig(1) }} to {{ as_.repeat_lift_high_pp | sig(1) }} pp) |
| 2 | **Fix delivery promises in the {{ a.state.n_target_states }} states above the national late rate** (carrier mix, realistic ETAs for the North-East and RJ) | These states receive {{ a.state.target_share_of_delivered | pct(0) }} of deliveries but {{ a.state.target_share_of_late | pct(0) }} of late orders; RJ alone holds {{ a.state.rj_share_of_late | pct(0) }}; late orders average {{ d.late_avg_review | sig }} stars | **{{ i.a2_late_orders_avoided | num }} fewer late orders a year** and +{{ i.a2_review_uplift | sig }} on the platform review average; direct repeat revenue is small ({{ i.a2_repeat_revenue | money(0) }}) |
| 3 | **Cross-sell to build baskets** in the top categories ("bought together" bundles at checkout) | Only {{ i.a3_multi_item_share | pct }} of orders have more than one item; an add-on is worth {{ i.a3_avg_addon_revenue | money }} on average | **{{ i.a3_basket_revenue | brl }}** if multi-item share rises {{ as_.basket_lift_pp | sig(1) }} pp |

Assumptions: annual volumes use the 2018 run-rate ({{ i.annual_new_customers | num }} new customers and {{ i.annual_orders | num }} orders a year);
a repeat order is worth {{ i.avg_repeat_order_revenue | money }} (observed average). The lifts are planning assumptions, not measured effects: run each action as an A/B test
before scaling. The impact model is live in `excel/olist_ecommerce_kpis.xlsx` (sheet *Impact model*) so the assumptions can be changed.

## Answers to the seven business questions

1. **Trend.** Revenue rose from {{ e.revenue_jan_2017 | brl }} in Jan 2017 to a Black-Friday peak of {{ e.peak_month_revenue | brl(2) }} in Nov 2017, then flattened.
   Average month-on-month growth fell from {{ t.avg_mom_growth_2017 | pct }} in 2017 to {{ t.avg_mom_growth_2018 | pct }} in 2018. The YoY gain is all volume
   (orders {{ t.yoy_orders_growth | pct(0) }}, AOV {{ t.yoy_aov_growth | pct }}).
   ![Monthly revenue](reports/figures/01_monthly_revenue.png)
2. **Categories and sellers.** The top 10 of {{ a.category.n_categories }} categories carry {{ a.category.top10_share | pct(0) }} of revenue, the bottom half only
   {{ a.category.bottom_half_share | pct }}. The top 10% of {{ a.category.n_sellers | num }} sellers earn {{ a.category.top10pct_sellers_share | pct(0) }}.
   Largest category: {{ a.category.top5[0].category | replace("_", " ") }} ({{ a.category.top5[0].share | pct }}).
3. **RFM segments.** Champions and Loyal (the only repeat buyers) are {{ a.rfm.repeat_segments_customer_share | pct }} of customers. Promising and At-Risk
   one-time buyers are the value pool: {{ a.rfm.high_value_one_timers_revenue_share | pct(0) }} of revenue.
   ![RFM segments](reports/figures/07_rfm_segments.png)
4. **Cohorts and repeat purchase.** No cohort retains more than {{ a.cohort.max_cell_retention_after_m0 | pct }} in any later month;
   {{ a.cohort.repeat_within_180d | pct }} of customers buy again within {{ as_.repeat_window_days }} days, and repeat orders are {{ a.cohort.repeat_revenue_share | pct }} of revenue.
   ![Cohort heatmap](reports/figures/08_cohort_heatmap.png)
5. **Late delivery vs review.** Late orders average {{ d.late_avg_review | sig }} stars against {{ d.ontime_avg_review | sig }} on time, a gap of
   {{ d.review_gap | sig }} stars ({{ as_.ci_level_pct }}% CI {{ d.review_gap_ci_low | sig }} to {{ d.review_gap_ci_high | sig }}). Mann-Whitney U: p < {{ as_.p_floor }},
   rank-biserial effect size {{ d.rank_biserial | sig }} (large). Orders 8+ days late average {{ d.late8_avg_review | sig }} stars and {{ d.late8_low_review_share | pct(0) }}
   of their reviews are 1-2 stars.
   ![Delay vs review](reports/figures/09_delay_vs_review.png)
6. **Logistics by state.** Worst late rates: {% for s in a.state.worst5[:3] %}{{ s.state }} {{ s.late_rate | pct }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}
   (national {{ k.late_rate | pct }}). RJ combines volume with a high rate ({{ a.state.rj_late_rate | pct }}): {{ a.state.rj_late_orders | num }} late orders,
   second only to SP ({{ a.state.sp_late_orders | num }}), whose rate is just {{ a.state.sp_late_rate | pct }}.
   ![State late rate](reports/figures/10_state_late_rate.png)
7. **Payment mix.** Credit card pays for {{ a.payment.credit_card_share | pct(0) }} of orders, boleto {{ a.payment.boleto_share | pct(0) }}. Credit-card order value rises with
   installments, from {{ a.payment.aov_single_payment | money }} (single payment) to {{ a.payment.aov_7plus_installments | money }} (7+), Spearman
   rho {{ a.payment.spearman_installments_value | sig }}. This is a correlation, not proof that installments cause bigger baskets.

