## Run it

```bash
python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate   # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
make all        # download -> warehouse -> audit -> clean -> eda -> analysis -> excel -> dashboard -> reports -> notebooks -> test -> check
```

`make all` rebuilds everything from the raw download. The data is fetched with `kagglehub` (no Kaggle login needed for this dataset) and falls
back to a public GitHub mirror. Raw files are not committed: the geolocation file alone is {{ q.geolocation_mb | sig(1) }} MB, and the licence restricts redistribution.

## Method notes

- **Scope.** Purchases from 2017-01-01 to 2018-08-31; canceled and unavailable orders removed. The {{ q.outside_window | num }} orders outside the window sit in partial months at the start and end of data collection.
- **Two independent implementations.** The SQL star schema and the pandas cleaning layer are written separately; tests require identical order counts, revenue, repeat rate and on-time rate.
- **RFM.** Recency and monetary use `NTILE(5)`. Frequency uses the order count, because {{ k.one_time_share | pct }} of customers have exactly one order and a frequency quintile would be meaningless.
- **Late delivery.** An order is late when delivered after the promised date (day grain). Effect size is reported because with {{ d.n_reviewed | num }} reviewed orders any difference is "significant".
- **Every number is generated.** README, summaries and dashboard text are rendered from `reports/facts.json`; `tools/check_numbers.py` fails the build if a number cannot be traced.

## Limitations

- No cost data: the dataset has prices and freight but no product cost, so this analyses revenue, not profit.
- Late first deliveries are linked to a lower repeat rate ({{ a.retention_vs_delivery.repeat_rate_late_first | pct(2) }} vs {{ a.retention_vs_delivery.repeat_rate_ontime_first | pct(2) }}, p = {{ a.retention_vs_delivery.p_value | sig(3) }}), but the gap is small: delivery is mainly a review-score problem, not the main reason customers do not return.
- Impact estimates use assumed lifts; they size the opportunity, they do not forecast it.

